feat(mcp,shim): record the run that started a subagent session (#214)

ADR-0045's third member had nowhere to go on this path. Before #208 the shim
registered subagents itself and could pass a parent; the rewrite moved
registration into observe_session and the argument was left behind, so every
session it registered was a root run.

What that cost was never the attribution — a subagent's work is attributed
either way. It is the EDGE. A reader of the ledger could see both runs and not
see that one produced the other, which is what "who did this work" resolves to
the moment an orchestrator delegates.

observe_session now takes an optional parent and threads it to register_agent
unchanged. It is not validated here: register_agent owns what a parent may be,
and a second opinion about it in this file is a second thing that can disagree
with the first. It is passed on every start including a duplicate, and is inert
on the second — register_agent replays the recorded reply for a key it has seen,
and a run id derives from (agent_type, task, idempotency_key) and not from the
parent, so a second start cannot move a run to a different parent.

ABSENT IS NOT AN ERROR AND IS NOT EMPTY. A session with no parent is a root run,
which is the ordinary case for an orchestrator's own session. register_agent
sets the member only when it is non-empty and mcp_call omits an empty value, so
a root run records no parent rather than claiming one it does not have — doc 02
§1 distinguishes the two, in a record nothing can amend.

THE SHIM HAD A SILENT BUG AND THE TEST IS THE ONLY REASON I KNOW. parent_run
first read $RUNS_DIR/$SESSION_ID; a session's marker is named `session-<id>`,
because subagent and session id spaces are not guaranteed disjoint — the same
reason IDENT exists beside KEY. Looking for the wrong filename finds nothing,
and finding nothing is indistinguishable from having no parent, so every
subagent would have stayed a root run against a shim that reads correctly.

Mutation-tested both halves. Removing the threading turns MCP-075 red and also
MCP-077, which is what proves MCP-077 refuses BECAUSE the parent reaches
validation rather than for an unrelated reason. MCP-076 stays green under that
mutation, correctly: a root run records no parent either way, so it is the
regression guard and not the detector.

The second harness's shim is left alone. Its transcript carries no parent, and
recording one there would be a relationship nobody reported.

Driven by MCP-075, MCP-076 and MCP-077, and by two new cases in the shim
self-test — one asserting the edge, one asserting an orphan subagent sends no
parent at all.

